{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>That was made at Mount Sinai.</b> Like that which was offered in the days of consecration (מלואים), before the Glory was transferred from Mount Sinai to the Tabernacle. And it appears from here that after Sinai, Israel did not offer burnt offerings at all in the wilderness. And in the first chapter of Ḥagigah, R. Elazar says: the daily burnt offering that was made at Mount Sinai — its procedure was stated from Sinai, but it itself was not offered. R. Akiva says: it was offered and never again ceased. But how do I uphold (Amos 5:25), ״הזבחים ומנחה הגשתם לי במדבר ארבעים שנה שבטו״ — did you offer sacrifices and meal offering to Me in the wilderness forty years, O house of Israel? — those of the tribe of Levi, who did not worship idolatry, did offer. Up to here. From this you learn that even according to R. Akiva, who says it was offered and never again ceased, Israel did not offer any burnt offerings in the wilderness; nonetheless, burnt offerings did not cease in the wilderness by means of the sons of Levi."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {0 "The prooftext from Amos is punctuated as a question following the Talmudic discussion (Ḥagigah 6b); the Hebrew שבטו is rendered per the Amos verse as שֶׁבֶּת יִשְׂרָאֵל ('O house of Israel')."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:36:41.441480408Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}